# Starting a New Prototype

## Mandatory sequence

**The sequence is always: Discovery → Figma → PRD → Task List → Setup → Build → Deploy. Never skip ahead.**

**Do NOT:**

- Run any commands or write any code before completing Steps 1–4
- Skip the Figma step if a link was provided
- Start building before the PRD and task list are both written and approved
- Ask more than the questions listed in Step 1 — collect everything in one shot

---

## Trigger

Run this flow whenever the user says anything like:

- "Start a prototype"
- "I want to make a prototype"
- "Start new prototype"
- "Create a prototype"
- "Let's build a prototype"

Do not wait for the user to give you commands. You drive the flow from start to finish.

---

## Step 1 — Discovery kickoff

Ask all of the following in **one message**. Do not proceed until the designer has answered.

> **Before we start building, I need to understand what we're making.**
>
> 1. **What's the prototype called?** (Short, no spaces — e.g. `coach-booking` or `session-review`)
> 2. **What are we building?** (One or two sentences — what is the flow or feature?)
> 3. **Who is the user in this prototype?** (e.g. player, coach, range operator)
> 4. **What's the one thing you most want to learn from testing this with a user?**
> 5. **Do you have a Figma link?** (Share any link — I'll pull the designs before we write the PRD)
> 6. **What is explicitly out of scope?** (Anything this prototype should NOT include)
> 7. **Does it need a backend, or is mock data enough?**

**STOP. Do not run any commands or write any code until the designer has answered.**

---

## Step 2 — Figma inspection (if a link was provided)

If the designer provided a Figma link in Step 1:

1. Use the Figma MCP (`get_design_context`) to fetch the design for the linked node
2. Read the designs and extract: layout, key screens, component patterns, copy, and any designer annotations
3. Summarise what you found in a short message to the designer:
   - Which screens / flows you can see (list the frame names — these become the candidate list of authoritative frames for the PRD)
   - Breakpoints present in the file (desktop / tablet / mobile)
   - Any motion or interaction annotations worth noting
   - Any design decisions or constraints worth noting
   - Anything unclear or missing from the designs
4. Ask:
   > "I've reviewed the Figma designs. Does this match what you want to build? Before I write the PRD, confirm: (a) which of these frames are authoritative for the build, (b) which breakpoints are in scope, and (c) whether motion/animations should be implemented."

Record the answers and carry them into the PRD's "Design Considerations" section (authoritative frames, Figma fidelity, breakpoints, motion).

Wait for the designer's confirmation before continuing.

If no Figma link was provided, skip this step and go straight to Step 3.

---

## Step 3 — Write the PRD

Follow `.cursor/templates/1. create-prd.md` to generate the PRD.

- Use the answers from Step 1 and the Figma inspection from Step 2 as your source material
- Do not ask additional clarifying questions unless something is genuinely ambiguous
- Save the PRD to `.cursor/documents/prds/` using the naming convention in the template

Once the PRD is written, share it with the designer and say:

> "Here's the PRD. Review it and let me know if anything needs changing — once you approve it, I'll generate the task list."

**STOP. Do not continue until the designer explicitly approves the PRD.**

---

## Step 4 — Generate the task list

Once the PRD is approved, read the PRD file you just saved and follow `.cursor/templates/2. generate-tasks.md` to generate the task list.

- Use the saved PRD as the sole source of truth for requirements — not the conversation history
- Save the task list to `.cursor/documents/tasks/` using the naming convention in the template
- Present the high-level tasks first and wait for "Go" before generating sub-tasks (as described in the template)

Once the full task list is written, say:

> "Here's the task list. Does this look right? Say **'start'** when you're ready and I'll set up the project and begin with Task 1."

**STOP. Do not run any commands until the designer says 'start' or equivalent.**

---

## Step 5 — Project setup

Only run this after the designer has approved the task list.

Run the following in sequence. Tell the user what you're doing at each step.

### 5a. Static site scaffold

Ensure the project has:

- `index.html` at the root with `<link>` to CSS and `<script type="module">` for `js/main.js`
- `css/styles.css` — global styles and `:root` variables (see design-system.mdc for brand alignment)
- `js/main.js` — bootstraps the UI
- `js/mockData.js` — mock data module

If the repo is this template, these files may already exist — extend them rather than replacing wholesale unless the PRD requires a different structure.

### 5b. Fonts

Use Google Fonts (or equivalent) in `index.html` for **Inter** (body) and **Oswald** (display). Wire `font-family` in CSS — see starter `index.html` / `css/styles.css` for the pattern.

### 5c. Mock data

Ensure `js/mockData.js` exists and exports realistic placeholder data; expand during build tasks.

### 5d. Start a local static server

ES modules require HTTP — do not rely on opening `index.html` via `file://`.

```bash
npx serve .
```

(Or another static server the environment supports.)

Tell the designer:

> "Setup is done — open the URL shown in the terminal (often http://localhost:3000). Starting Task 1 now."

---

## Step 6 — Build

Follow `.cursor/templates/3. process-task-list.md` for the full task execution protocol. Key rules:

- Before starting, read the saved PRD and task list files — use them as the source of truth throughout the build, not the conversation history
- All active Cursor rules apply as normal during the build (design-system guidance, fonts, mock data conventions, prototype scope, etc.)
- Work through tasks one at a time
- After each **parent task** completes, check the browser and report back:
  > "Task [n] is done — here's what was built: [short summary]. Want a quick review before I move on, or shall I go straight to Task [n+1]?"
- Do not start the next task until the designer says yes
- If the Figma link was provided, refer back to the designs throughout to validate visual accuracy

---

## Step 7 — Wrap-up

When all tasks in the task list are complete, say:

> "The prototype is done. Here's what was built: [brief summary of screens/flows]."
>
> **What next?**
> - Say **'deploy'** and I'll publish it to Vercel and share a link
> - Say **'tweak [thing]'** and I'll make adjustments before we ship

---

## Rules for this flow

- Never ask the designer to open a terminal
- Run all commands yourself
- If a command fails, fix it and retry silently before telling the user
- From Step 4 onwards, always read the saved PRD and task list files at the start of each step — never rely on conversation history as the source of requirements
- All `.cursor/rules/` apply throughout the entire flow — this workflow does not override them, it sequences them
- Refer to `prototype-scope.mdc` throughout for what to build and what to skip
- If the designer starts describing what to build before the PRD is approved, collect their input but redirect: use it as additional input to the PRD rather than starting to build
